WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:24.080
<v Ira Glass>Alex Meyer wants my job. He's 16, and he wants my job. But nobody is going to give him my job, or really any job in radio. And so last year he decided to create his own radio show as a high school freshman in Seattle. And he did it with the kind of devotion and attention to detail of a true believer. It's kind of an amazing show actually, starting with the opening, which he lifted from one of his radio heroes, Tom Leykis, who does a syndicated show out of Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·梅耶想要我的工作。他16岁，他想要我的工作。但没人会把我的工作给他，或者说，在广播行业，没人会给他任何工作。所以去年，身为西雅图高中一年级学生的他，决定创办自己的广播节目。他带着那种真正信徒般的虔诚和对细节的关注去做了这件事。实际上，这个节目相当令人惊叹，尤其是开场，他直接沿用了他的一位广播偶像——汤姆·莱基斯，那位在洛杉矶主持联合广播节目的播音员。

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:50.290
<v Alex Meyer>I was pretty much ripping off Leykis, because at the top of every hour he plays Metallica, "Enter Sandman." And he has this big thing. They're all saying, "Live from Los Angeles, it's the Tom Leykis Show." And he's like "Thank you for tuning into the Tom Leykis show. It's not hosted by a right wing wacko, a convicted felon. No, I'm your host." Blah, blah, blah, blah. And gives out the numbers and everything. So I was like, I want something like that.
<v Alex Meyer>我基本上是剽窃莱基斯的，因为他每个整点都会播放Metallica乐队的《Enter Sandman》。他还有个标志性的开场白，他们都在说：“这是洛杉矶直播的汤姆·莱基斯秀。”他说：“感谢收听汤姆·莱基斯秀。主持人不是什么右翼怪胎或刑满释放犯。不，我是你们的主持人。”等等，等等。然后报出电话号码之类的。所以我当时就想，我也要那种感觉。

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:50.660
<v Ira Glass>Yeah that sounds good.
<v Ira Glass>是的，听起来不错。

00:00:50.660 --> 00:01:25.450
<v Alex Meyer>I want my big thing. "Hey everybody. It's The Alex Meyer Show. 1-800-421-ALEX, toll-free. Yeah that's free. 1-800-421-2539--" Actually, if you call that number, it's AT&T. And I think they ask you if you want it in Spanish. In Seattle, a lot of the radio station numbers start with 421. That's something I noticed. And put 421, put ALEX, 1-800. Oh, sounds good.
<v Alex Meyer>我要有自己的标志性开场。“大家好，这是亚历克斯·梅耶秀。1-800-421-ALEX，免费电话。是的，免费的。1-800-421-2539——”其实如果你拨打那个号码，那是AT&T的电话。我想他们会问你是否需要西班牙语服务。在西雅图，很多广播电台的电话号码都是以421开头的。这是我注意到的。加上421，加上ALEX，1-800。噢，听起来不错。

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.240
<v Ira Glass>Now sometimes you have your friend Matt come on and--
<v Ira Glass>现在有时你会让你的朋友马特上节目，然后——

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:41.930
<v Alex Meyer>Yeah, my quote, "producer." I gave him that title, because I needed a producer character to pretty much yell at. Because you hear that a lot on the radio here, dysfunctional radio shows. So I figured, wow. He's pretty much just a character.
<v Alex Meyer>是的，我所谓的“制作人”。我给了他这个头衔，因为我需要一个制作人角色来让我对他大吼大叫。因为你在收音机里经常听到那种功能失调的广播节目。所以我心想，哇，他基本上只是个角色。

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:57.630
<v Alex Meyer>"Well we have Matt the producer here to talk to you about this actually. This is Matt's area of expertise. It's something that happened to him. His ex-girlfriend pretty much wanted him for the dance. It almost seems like--
<v Alex Meyer>“好吧，关于这一点，我们请来了制作人马特和大家谈谈。这是马特的专业领域。这是发生在他身上的事。他的前女友基本上是为了舞会才找他的。这看起来好像——”

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.190
<v Matt>That's what it seems like.
<v Matt>看起来确实是这样。

00:01:58.190 --> 00:01:59.670
<v Alex Meyer>She used him for the dance.
<v Alex Meyer>她利用他参加了舞会。

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.450
<v Matt>That's what it seems like.
<v Matt>看起来确实是这样。

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:05.540
<v Alex Meyer>And I'll tell you something. A while ago, she asked me to the next dance.
<v Alex Meyer>跟你说件事。不久前，她邀请我参加下一场舞会。

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.250
<v Matt>Wow.
<v Matt>哇。

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:18.140
<v Alex Meyer>And see, I never really wanted to go with her. First I told her I was building houses for poor people in Mexico, and therefore would not be able to make it.
<v Alex Meyer>你看，我从来没想过和她一起去。起初我告诉她我正在墨西哥为穷人盖房子，所以没法参加。

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:39.780
<v Ira Glass>What's remarkable is that even though Alex is making the show in his room on his dad's discarded cassette recorder, and even though the show never actually broadcasts anywhere, it sounds like a real commercial radio talk show. The pacing, the fact that Alex is closer to the mic than any of his guests so he sounds clearer and louder. He has phone callers, though of course, since he's not broadcasting, Alex plays those himself.
<v Ira Glass>值得注意的是，尽管亚历克斯是在他的房间里用他父亲扔掉的盒式录音机制作这个节目，而且这个节目实际上从未在任何地方播出过，但它听起来就像真正的商业谈话电台节目。节奏感，还有亚历克斯比他任何嘉宾都更靠近麦克风，所以他的声音听起来更清晰、更大声。他有电话听众，当然，因为他没有播出，所以这些电话都是亚历克斯自己假装的。

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.980
<v Alex Meyer>And I have Dan from Eugene.
<v Alex Meyer>现在我有来自尤金的丹。

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.530
<v Ira Glass>Dan is an irritating character.
<v Ira Glass>丹是一个让人讨厌的角色。

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.950
<v Alex Meyer>Yeah, I had to have the guy who hated me. And he's that character.
<v Alex Meyer>是的，我必须得有个讨厌我的人。他就是那个角色。

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:50.400
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.480
<v Alex Meyer>So every time he calls, I'm, Alex, I hate your show. You suck. This is Dan from Eugene.
<v Alex Meyer>所以每次他打来电话，我就会说，亚历克斯，我讨厌你的节目。你烂透了。我是来自尤金的丹。

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:03.670
<v Ira Glass>I love how part of the formula of having a fantasy radio show is that you have a fantasy person who hates your guts.
<v Ira Glass>我喜欢这种奇幻广播节目的套路，就是你得有一个虚构的人物来恨之入骨。

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.030
<v Alex Meyer>Well, it's kind of fun being someone that hates you though. It is kind of fun.
<v Alex Meyer>嗯，做一个恨你的人其实挺有趣的。真的挺有趣的。

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:33.370
<v Ira Glass>To make it sound as real as possible, Alex uses real commercials that he tapes off the radio. Sometimes he and Matt pretend to have a seven second delay, like Howard Stern and the other big shows use. And someone has said a bad word, so what you hear is this sudden gap and jump in content, though of course, it's just a cassette in his bedroom. It's not a real broadcast. And like any modern ambitious talk show host, there are right wing rants. That's just part of the formula.
<v Ira Glass>为了让节目听起来尽可能真实，亚历克斯使用了他从广播里录下来的真实广告。有时他和马特会假装有七秒的延迟，就像霍华德·斯特恩和其他大节目那样。如果有人说了脏话，你听到的就是内容突然的中断和跳跃，当然，这只是他卧室里的磁带。这并不是真正的广播。而且像任何有抱负的现代谈话节目主持人一样，节目里也有右翼的咆哮。这只是套路的一部分。

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.350
<v Alex Meyer>You know what makes no sense at all.
<v Alex Meyer>你知道什么是最没道理的吗。

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.390
<v Matt>A dollar bill--
<v Matt>一张一美元纸币——

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:44.690
<v Alex Meyer>For me to pass high school, according to the state, I need to learn Spanish. Yet--
<v Alex Meyer>对我来说，要通过高中，根据州的规定，我必须学习西班牙语。然而——

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:45.430
<v Matt>Or any foreign language.
<v Matt>或者任何外语。

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:53.230
<v Alex Meyer>Wait, wait. But yet, foreigners working in 7-Elevens don't have to know a god damn word of English.
<v Alex Meyer>等等，等等。然而，在7-Eleven工作的外国人根本不需要会说哪怕一个英语单词。

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:53.630
<v Matt>No they don't.
<v Matt>是的，他们不需要。

00:03:53.630 --> 00:04:01.990
<v Alex Meyer>What sense does that make? For me to live in this country, I have to learn a foreign language. They don't have to learn anything.
<v Alex Meyer>这有什么道理？为了住在这个国家，我必须学习一门外语。他们却什么都不用学。

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:15.790
<v Ira Glass>I wondered if you're more like a real radio personality or shock jock, where that's a thought that, actually, you only half believe that. But your views aren't quite as extreme as you're saying in the recording.
<v Ira Glass>我很好奇，你更像是一个真正的广播个性和“震惊电台”主持人吗？那种想法其实你只信了一半，但你的观点并不像你在录音里说得那么极端。

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.930
<v Alex Meyer>Yeah pretty much. I mean it's more of an shtick thing, I guess.
<v Alex Meyer>是的，差不多。我的意思是，那更多是一种噱头，我想。

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:44.260
<v Ira Glass>Alex distributes his show by copying it onto CDs for friends at school. Big ratings for him, he says, are when he gets an audience of six, six people. But frankly, having people listen doesn't seem to be the important part of it to him. He's in training. No one seems to be interested giving a 16 year old a job in radio or a place to learn. So he created his own place where he's learning, honing his skills, preparing for the future, which brings me to the subject of today's radio show.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯通过为学校的朋友复制CD来传播他的节目。他说，当他有六个听众时，那就是极高的收视率了。但坦率地说，有没有人听对他来说似乎并不重要。他正在受训。似乎没人有兴趣给一个16岁的孩子一份广播工作或一个学习的地方。所以他创造了自己的天地，在那里他进行学习，磨练技能，为未来做准备，这让我引出了今天广播节目的主题。

00:04:44.260 --> 00:05:11.230
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, we bring you three stories of people who take on unlikely tasks, put themselves in charge in very unpromising circumstances. And we see what happens to them as a result. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. Act One of today's show, It's Not Just the Aces That Are Wild. In that act, David Sedaris tells the story of how as a teenager he was scared of certain people until he made them scared of him.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们为您带来三个关于人们承担不可能的任务，在极不乐观的情况下掌舵的故事。我们来看看结果如何。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《这就是美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。今天节目的第一幕，“不仅仅是王牌大显身手”。在这一幕中，大卫·塞达里斯讲述了他十几岁时如何害怕某些人，直到他让他们反过来害怕他。

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:40.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Playing Clinton in the Bush Leagues, in which we bring you the story of a politician with tons of charisma, full of promise, who goes through the fastest rise and fall we have ever heard of in politics. Act Three, You Talking to Me? Yes, we all know we Americans declared ourselves the rulers of Iraq for now. So why is one of the most popular rumors in the country that Saddam Hussein is not only alive, but living in the White House? Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“在低级别联赛中扮演克林顿”，我们为你讲述一个极具魅力、前途无量的政客的故事，他经历了我们所听过的政治领域中最快的起起落落。第三幕，“你在跟我说话吗？”是的，我们都知道，美国人现在宣布自己是伊拉克的统治者。那么，为什么国内最流行的谣言之一是萨达姆·侯赛因不仅活着，而且住在白宫里呢？请继续收听。

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:48.800
<v Ira Glass>And let's get right to it with Act One. Here's writer David Sedaris, recorded in front of a live audience in Boston.
<v Ira Glass>让我们直接进入第一幕。这里是作家大卫·塞达里斯，录制于波士顿的现场观众面前。

00:05:48.800 --> 00:06:26.300
<v David Sedaris>My parents were not the type of people who went to bed at a regular hour. Sleep overtook them, but neither the time nor the idea of a mattress seemed very important. My father favored a chair in the basement, but my mother was apt to lie down anywhere, waking with carpet burns on her face or the pattern of the sofa embossed into the soft flesh of her upper arms. It was sort of embarrassing. She might sleep for eight hours a day. But they were never consecutive hours, and they involved no separate outfit.
<v David Sedaris>我的父母不是那种会在固定时间上床睡觉的人。睡意会袭向他们，但睡眠时间和床垫对他们来说似乎都不怎么重要。我父亲喜欢地下室的一把椅子，但我母亲随时随地都能躺下，醒来时脸上带着地毯的压痕，或是沙发的花纹印在她上臂柔软的皮肤上。那有点尴尬。她每天可能会睡八个小时。但那从来不是连续的时间，而且也不需要什么特别的打扮。

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:46.270
<v David Sedaris>For Christmas, we would give her nightgowns, hoping she might take the hint. They're for bedtime, we'd say. Then she'd look at us strangely as if, like the moment of one's death, the occasion of sleep was too incalculable for any real preparation.
<v David Sedaris>圣诞节时，我们会送她睡袍，希望她能领会暗示。我们会说，这是睡觉用的。然后她会奇怪地看着我们，仿佛睡眠的场合就像人死亡的时刻一样，过于难以预料，以至于无法做任何真正的准备。

00:06:46.270 --> 00:07:21.710
<v David Sedaris>The upswing to being raised by what were essentially a pair of house cats was that we never had any enforced bedtime. We were the family that never shut down, the family whose TV was so hot we needed an oven mitt in order to change the channels. Every night was basically a slumber party. So when the real thing came along, my sisters and I failed to show much of an interest.
<v David Sedaris>被一对本质上是家猫的父母抚养长大的好处是，我们从没有强制的就寝时间。我们是一个从不“关机”的家庭，我们家的电视热得我们需要用烤箱手套才能换台。每个夜晚基本上都是睡衣派对。所以当真正的睡衣派对到来时，我和我的姐妹们并没有表现出太大的兴趣。

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:54.750
<v David Sedaris>The first one I attended was held by a neighbor named Walt Winters. Like myself, Walt was in the sixth grade. Unlike myself, he was gregarious and athletic, which meant basically that we had absolutely nothing in common. "But why would he include me?" I asked my mother. "I hardly know the guy." She did not say that Walt's mother had made him invite me, but on watching her turn away, I knew that this was the only likely explanation. "Oh go," she said, "it'll be fun."
<v David Sedaris>我参加的第一个派对是由一位叫沃尔特·温特斯的邻居举办的。和我一样，沃尔特也在上六年级。不像我，他性格开朗且热爱运动，这意味着我们完全没有共同点。“但他为什么要邀请我？”我问母亲。“我几乎不认识他。”她没说沃尔特是因为他母亲逼他才邀请我的，但看着她转身的样子，我知道这才是唯一可能的解释。“哦，去吧，”她说，“会很有趣的。”

00:07:54.750 --> 00:08:33.080
<v David Sedaris>There were four styles of houses on our street, and while Walt's was different from my own, I was familiar with the layout. The slumber party took place in what the Methodists called the family room, the Catholics used as an extra bedroom, and the neighborhood's only Jews had turned into a combination dark room and fallout shelter. Walt's family was Methodist, and so the room's focal point was a large black and white television. Family photos hung on the wall alongside pictures of the various athletes Mr. Winters had successfully pestered for autographs.
<v David Sedaris>我们街上有四种户型的房子，虽然沃尔特的房子和我自己的不同，但我对布局很熟悉。睡衣派对是在卫理公会教徒称之为“家庭活动室”的地方举行的，天主教徒用它做额外的卧室，而邻里唯一的犹太人则把它变成了一个暗室和防空洞的结合体。沃尔特的家人是卫理公会教徒，所以房间的焦点是一台巨大的黑白电视。墙上挂着家庭照片，旁边还有温特斯先生从各种运动员那里厚着脸皮要来的签名照。

00:08:33.080 --> 00:09:18.910
<v David Sedaris>I admired them to the best of my ability but was more interested in the wedding portrait displayed above the sofa. Arm in arm with her uniformed husband, Walt's mother looked deliriously, almost frighteningly happy. The bulging eyes and fierce, gummy smile. It was an expression bordering on hysteria, and the intervening years had done nothing to dampen it. On passing Mrs. Winters waving gaily from her front yard, my mother would whisper, "What is she on?" I thought she was being too hard on her, but after 10 minutes in the woman's home, I understood exactly what my mother was talking about.
<v David Sedaris>我尽我所能地赞美它们，但我对沙发上方展示的结婚照更感兴趣。沃尔特的母亲挽着穿制服的丈夫，看起来神魂颠倒，几乎令人恐惧地幸福。那凸出的眼睛和凶狠的、露着牙龈的笑容。那是一种近乎歇斯底里的表情，随后的几年里，这种表情从未减弱过。当看到温特斯太太在院子里欢快地挥手时，我母亲会低声说：“她到底吃了什么？”我觉得她对他太苛刻了，但在那个女人的家里待了10分钟后，我完全明白了我母亲在说什么。

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:48.360
<v David Sedaris>"Pizza's here," she chimed when the delivery man came to the door. "Oh boys, how about some piping hot pizza?" I thought it was funny that anyone would use the words piping hot. But it wasn't the kind of thing I felt I could actually laugh at. Neither could I laugh at Mr. Winters' pathetic imitation of an Italian waiter. "Mamma Mia, who want another slice of the pizza?"
<v David Sedaris>“披萨到了，”当送货员到门口时，她欢快地说道。“噢，孩子们，来点热气腾腾的披萨怎么样？”我觉得一个人用“热气腾腾”这个词很有趣。但我并不觉得那是我能真正笑出来的东西。我也无法对温特斯先生那蹩脚的意大利侍者模仿秀发笑。“妈妈咪呀，谁还想再来一片披萨？”

00:09:48.360 --> 00:10:31.490
<v David Sedaris>I had the idea that adults were supposed to make themselves scarce at slumber parties. But Walt's parents were all over the place, initiating games, offering snacks and refills. When the midnight horror movie came on, Walt's mother crept into the bathroom, leaving a ketchup-spattered knife beside the sink. An hour passed, and when none of us had yet discovered it, she started dropping little hints. "Doesn't anyone want to wash their hands?" she asked. "Will whoever is closest to the door go check and see if I left fresh towels in the bathroom?" You just wanted to cry for people like her.
<v David Sedaris>我的印象是，成年人在睡衣派对上应该离得远远的。但沃尔特的父母到处都是，发起游戏，提供零食和续杯。当午夜恐怖电影开始时，沃尔特的母亲溜进洗手间，在洗手池旁留下了一把沾着番茄酱的刀。一个小时过去了，当我们还没人发现它时，她开始给出小小的暗示。“没人想洗手吗？”她问。“最靠近门的那个人去检查一下我是否在洗手间留了干净的毛巾好吗？”你只会为她这样的人感到想哭。

00:10:31.490 --> 00:11:00.040
<v David Sedaris>As corny as they were, I was sorry when the movie ended and Mr. And Mrs. Winters stood to leave. It was only 2:00 AM, but clearly they were done in. "I just don't know how you boys can do it," Walt's mother said, yawning into the sleeve of her bathrobe. "I haven't been up this late since Lauren came into the world. Lauren was Walt's sister, who was born premature and had lived for less than two days.
<v David Sedaris>虽然它们很俗气，但当电影结束，温特斯夫妇起身离开时，我还是感到遗憾。才凌晨2点，但显然他们已经累坏了。“我真不知道你们这些小伙子是怎么做到的，”沃尔特母亲打着哈欠，对着浴袍袖子说，“我自从劳伦出生以来就没熬过这么晚。”劳伦是沃尔特的妹妹，早产，只活了不到两天。

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:46.310
<v David Sedaris>This had happened before the Winters moved onto our street, but it wasn't any kind of a secret. And you weren't supposed to flinch upon hearing the girl's name. The baby had died too early to pose for photographs, but still she was regarded as a full fledged member of the family. She had a Christmas stocking the size of a mitten. And they even threw her an annual birthday party, a fact my mother found especially creepy. "Let's hope they don't invite us," she said. "I mean Jesus, how do you shop for a dead baby?"
<v David Sedaris>这发生在温特斯一家搬到我们街上之前，但这并不是什么秘密。你被要求在听到这个女孩的名字时不能畏缩。那个婴儿死得太早，还没来得及拍照，但她仍然被视为家庭的正式成员。她有一个手套大小的圣诞袜。他们甚至每年都会为她举办生日派对，我母亲觉得这一点尤其令人毛骨悚然。“希望他们别邀请我们，”她说，“我的意思是，天哪，你怎么给一个死婴买礼物？”

00:11:46.310 --> 00:12:34.880
<v David Sedaris>I guessed it was the fear of another premature birth that kept Mrs. Winters from trying again, which was sad, as you got the sense she really wanted a lively household. You got the sense she had an idea of a lively household, and that the slumber party and the ketchup-covered knife were all a part of that idea. While in her presence, we had played along. But once she said good night, I understood that all bets were off. She and her husband lumbered up the stairs. And when Walt felt certain they were asleep, he pounced on Dale Gummerson, shouting "Titty twister!" Brad Clancy joined in, and when they had finished, Dale raised his shirt, revealing nipples as crimped and ruddy as the pepperoni slices littering the forsaken pizza box.
<v David Sedaris>我猜正是对另一次早产的恐惧让温特斯太太没再尝试生育，这很令人难过，因为你能感觉到她真的很想要一个热闹的家庭。你能感觉到她对热闹家庭有个概念，而睡衣派对和沾着番茄酱的刀都是那个概念的一部分。在她在场时，我们配合着表演。但当她道过晚安后，我知道一切都不一样了。她和丈夫笨重地走上楼梯。当沃尔特确定他们睡着后，他猛扑向戴尔·古默森，大喊：“乳头扭转！”布拉德·克兰西也加入了，当他们完成后，戴尔掀起衬衫，露出的乳头就像撒在废弃披萨盒上的意大利辣香肠片一样皱巴巴、红彤彤的。

00:12:34.880 --> 00:13:06.930
<v David Sedaris>"Oh my God," I said, realizing too late that this made me sound like a girl. The appropriate response was to laugh at Dale's misfortune, not to flutter your hands in front of your face, screeching, "What have they done to your poor nipples? Shouldn't we put some ice on them?"
<v David Sedaris>“噢，天哪，”我说，意识到太晚了，这让我听起来像个女孩。适当的反应应该是嘲笑戴尔的倒霉，而不是在脸前扇动手，尖叫着：“他们对你可怜的乳头做了什么？我们不该放点冰块上去吗？”

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:29.780
<v David Sedaris>Walt picked up on this immediately. "Did you just say you wanted to put ice on Dale's nipples?" "Well not me personally," I said. "I meant, you know, generally, as a group. Or Dale could do it himself if he felt like it."
<v David Sedaris>沃尔特立刻抓住了这一点。“你刚才说你想给戴尔的乳头放点冰块？”“好吧，不是我个人，”我说，“我的意思是，你知道，作为一个群体来说。或者如果戴尔愿意，他可以自己放。”

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:57.360
<v David Sedaris>Walt's eyes wandered from my face to my chest. And then the entire slumber party was upon me. My shirt was raised, a hand was clamped over my mouth, and Walt latched on to my nipples, twisting them back and forth as if they were a set of particularly stubborn lug nuts. "Now who needs ice?" he said. "Now who thinks he's a god damn school nurse?"
<v David Sedaris>沃尔特的目光从我的脸移到我的胸部。接着整个睡衣派对的人都冲向了我。我的衬衫被掀起，一只手捂住我的嘴，沃尔特紧紧抓住我的乳头，来回扭动，就像它们是一组特别难搞的螺母。“现在谁需要冰块？”他说，“现在谁以为自己是该死的校医？”

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:11.730
<v David Sedaris>I'd once felt sorry for Walt, but now, my eyes watering in pain, I understood that little Lauren was smart to have cut out early.
<v David Sedaris>我曾经为沃尔特感到遗憾，但现在，我疼得眼泪直流，我明白小劳伦早点离开是明智的。

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:39.000
<v David Sedaris>When eventually I was freed, I went upstairs and stood at the kitchen window, my arms folded lightly against my chest. My house was located in a ravine. You couldn't see it from the street, but still I could make out the glow of light spilling from the top of our driveway. It was tempting, but were I to leave now, I'd never hear the end of it. The baby cried. The baby had to go home. Life at school would be unbearable.
<v David Sedaris>当我最终获释后，我走到楼上，站在厨房窗前，双臂轻轻交叠在胸前。我家位于山谷中。从街上看不见它，但我仍能分辨出从我家车道顶端透出的灯光。这很诱人，但如果我现在离开，我将永远听不到尽头。婴儿哭了。婴儿必须回家。学校的生活将变得无法忍受。

00:14:39.000 --> 00:15:15.020
<v David Sedaris>And so I left the window and returned to the basement where Walt was shuffling cards against the coffee table. "Just in time," he said. "Have a seat." I lowered myself to the floor and reached for a magazine. "I'm not really much for games. So if it's OK with you, I think I'll just watch." "Watch hell," Walt said. "This is strip poker. What kind of a homo wants to sit around and watch four guys get naked?"
<v David Sedaris>于是我离开窗户回到地下室，沃尔特正在咖啡桌上洗牌。“来得正好，”他说，“坐下。”我坐到地板上，伸手拿过一本杂志。“我对游戏不太感兴趣。所以如果你们不介意，我想我只是看看。”“看个屁，”沃尔特说，“这是脱衣扑克。有哪个同性恋想坐在这里看四个家伙脱光衣服？”

00:15:15.020 --> 00:16:10.120
<v David Sedaris>The logic of this was lost on me. "Well won't we all sort of be watching?" "Looking maybe, but not watching," Walt said. "There's a difference." Again, I had no idea what he was talking about. Walt made a twisting motion with his fingers, and I took my place at the table, praying for a gas leak or an electrical fire, anything to save me from the catastrophe of strip poker. To the rest of the group, a naked boy was like a lamp or an extension cord, something so familiar and uninteresting it faded into the background. But for me it was different. A naked boy was what I desired more than anything on earth. And when you were both watching and desiring, things came up, one thing, in particular, that was bound to stand out and ruin your life forever.
<v David Sedaris>我完全没听懂这其中的逻辑。“好吧，难道我们不都是在看吗？”“可能是在看，但不是在‘观看’，”沃尔特说，“这有区别。”再一次，我不知道他在说什么。沃尔特用手指做了个扭转动作，我在桌边坐下，祈祷发生煤气泄漏或电气火灾，任何能让我从脱衣扑克的灾难中解救出来的东西。对于其他人来说，一个裸体男孩就像一盏灯或一根延长线，熟悉且毫无趣味，以至于它会淡入背景。但对我来说不同。一个裸体男孩是我在地球上最渴望的东西。当你既在观看又在渴望时，事情就会发生，特别是某样东西，注定会突显出来并永远毁了你的生活。

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:44.200
<v David Sedaris>"I hate to tell you," I said, "but it's against my religion to play poker." "Yeah right," Walt said. "What are you Baptists?" "Greek Orthodox." "Well then that's a load of crap, because the Greeks invented cards," Walt said. "Actually, I think it was the Egyptians." This from Scott, who was quickly identifying himself as the smart one. "Greeks, Egyptians, they're all the same thing," Walt said. "Anyway, what your pooh-bah doesn't know won't hurt him. So shut the hell up and play."
<v David Sedaris>“我不想告诉你，”我说，“但玩扑克违背了我的宗教信仰。”“是啊，真的，”沃尔特说，“你们是浸信会吗？”“希腊正教。”“那简直是一派胡言，因为扑克牌是希腊人发明的，”沃尔特说。“其实，我认为是埃及人发明的。”这是斯科特说的，他很快就把自己定义为聪明人。“希腊人，埃及人，他们都是一回事，”沃尔特说，“总之，你们那教皇不知道的事不会伤害到他。所以闭上臭嘴，开始玩。”

00:16:44.200 --> 00:17:19.020
<v David Sedaris>He dealt the cards, and I looked from face to face, exaggerating flaws and reminding myself that these boys did not like me. The hope was that I might kill any surviving atom of attraction. But as has been the case for my entire life, the more someone dislikes me, the more attractive they become. The key was to stall, to argue every hand until the sun came up and Mrs. Winters saved me with whatever cheerful monstrosity she had planned for breakfast.
<v David Sedaris>他发了牌，我看着每一张脸，夸大他们的缺点，提醒自己这些男孩不喜欢我。希望我能以此杀灭任何残留的吸引力。但正如我一生中所经历的那样，某人越不喜欢我，他们就越有吸引力。关键在于拖延，在每一手牌上争论，直到太阳升起，温特斯太太用她计划好的任何欢快的怪物早餐来拯救我。

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:49.970
<v David Sedaris>Usually when forced to compete, it was my tactic to simply give up. To try in any way was to announce your ambition, which only made you more vulnerable. Here though, surrender was not an option. I had to win in a game I knew nothing about. And that seemed hopeless until I realized we were all on an even keel. Not even Scott had the slightest idea what he was doing. And by feigning an air of expertise, I found I could manipulate things in my favor.
<v David Sedaris>通常在被迫竞争时，我的策略就是直接放弃。尝试任何方式都是在宣告你的野心，那只会让你变得更脆弱。但在这里，投降不是选项。我必须在一场我一无所知的游戏中获胜。这看起来毫无希望，直到我意识到我们都在同一起跑线上。即使是斯科特也完全不知道自己在做什么。通过假装专家，我发现我可以操纵局势对我有利。

00:17:49.970 --> 00:18:21.310
<v David Sedaris>"A joker and a queen is much better than a four and five of spades," I said, defending my hand against Brad Clancy's. "But you have a joker and a three of diamonds." "Yeah, but the joker makes it a queen." "I thought you said that poker was against your religion," Walt said. "Well that doesn't mean I don't understand it. Greeks invented cards, remember? They're in my blood."
<v David Sedaris>“一张小丑牌加一张皇后牌比一张4加一张5的黑桃好多了，”我说，为我的手牌对抗布拉德·克兰西的牌做辩护。“但你有一张小丑牌和一张3的方块。”“是啊，但小丑牌让它变成了一张皇后。”“我以为你说扑克违背了你的宗教信仰，”沃尔特说。“好吧，这并不意味着我不懂它。希腊人发明了扑克牌，记得吗？它们流淌在我的血液里。”

00:18:21.310 --> 00:19:07.420
<v David Sedaris>At the start of the game, the starburst clock had read 3:30. An hour later I was missing one shoe. Scott and Brad had lost their shirts. And both Walt and Dale were down to their underwear. If this was what winning felt like, I wondered why I hadn't tried it earlier. Confidently in the lead, I invented little reasons for the undressed to get up and move about the room. "Hey Walt, did you hear that? Sounded like your mother calling." "I didn't hear anything." "Well why don't you go to the stairway and check? We don't want any surprises." His underwear was all bunchy in the back, saggy like a diaper. But his legs were meaty and satisfying to look at.
<v David Sedaris>比赛开始时，星爆钟显示3点半。一小时后，我少了一只鞋。斯科特和布拉德输掉了衬衫。沃尔特和戴尔只剩下内裤。如果这就是赢的感觉，我纳闷为什么我不早点尝试。自信地处于领先地位，我发明了一些小借口让那些脱了衣服的人站起来四处走动。“嘿沃尔特，你听到了吗？听起来像你妈妈在叫你。”“我没听到什么。”“好吧，你为什么不去楼梯口检查一下？我们可不想有什么意外。”他的内裤在后面挤成一团，松垮垮像尿布。但他的腿很有肉感，看起来很满足。

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:32.890
<v David Sedaris>"Dale, would you make sure those curtains are closed?" He crossed the room, and I ate him alive with my eyes, confident that no one would accuse me of gaping. Things might have been different were I in last place. But as the winner, it was my right to make sure that things were done properly. "There's a little gap down by the baseboard. Bend over and close it, will you?"
<v David Sedaris>“戴尔，你能确保窗帘拉上了吗？”他走过房间，我用眼睛把他生吞活剥了，自信没有人会指责我盯着他看。如果我排在最后，情况可能就不同了。但作为赢家，我有权确保事情做得妥当。“踢脚板那里有个小缝。弯腰把它关上，好吗？”

00:19:32.890 --> 00:20:20.370
<v David Sedaris>It took a while, but once I explained that a pair of kings was no match for the two of hearts and a three of spades, Walt surrendered his underpants and tossed them onto the pile beside the TV set. "OK," he said, "now the rest of you can finish the game." "But it is finished," Scott said. "Oh no," Walt said. "I'm not the only one getting naked. You guys have to keep playing." "While you what? Sit and watch?" I said. "What kind of a homo are you?" "Yeah," Dale said. "Why don't we do something else? This game's boring and the rules are impossible."
<v David Sedaris>花了一些时间，但一旦我解释了两个K根本比不过两张红心和一张3的黑桃，沃尔特交出了他的内裤，扔在电视机旁的那堆衣服上。“好了，”他说，“现在你们其他人可以把比赛结束了。”“但已经结束了，”斯科特说。“哦，不，”沃尔特说，“不仅仅是我一个人要脱光。你们这些家伙必须继续玩。”“然后你干嘛？坐着看？”我说，“你是什么样的同性恋？”“是啊，”戴尔说，“我们为什么不干点别的？这游戏真无聊，规则也根本不可能。”

00:20:20.370 --> 00:21:03.870
<v David Sedaris>The others muttered their agreement. And when Walt refused to back down, I gathered the deck and tamped it commandingly upon the table top. "The only solution is for us all to keep playing." "Well how the hell do you expect me to do that?" Walt said. "In case you haven't noticed, there's nothing more for me to lose." "Oh," I said, "there's always more. Maybe if the weakest hand is already naked, we should make that person perform some kind of a task. Nothing big, but you know, just a token kind of a thing." "A think like what?" Walt asked. "I don't know. I guess we'll just have to cross that bridge when we come to it."
<v David Sedaris>其他人嘀咕着表示同意。当沃尔特拒绝退让时，我收集起牌组，威严地在桌面上敲了敲。“唯一的解决方案就是我们所有人都继续玩。”“那你到底想让我怎么玩？”沃尔特说，“如果你没注意到的话，我已经没什么可输的了。”“哦，”我说，“总还有更多的。也许如果手牌最差的人已经脱光了，我们应该让那个人执行某种任务。没什么大不了，你知道，就当个象征性的事。”“什么样的任务？”沃尔特问。“我不知道。我想我们得等遇到那座桥的时候再说。”

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:41.070
<v David Sedaris>In retrospect, I probably went a little too far in ordering Scott to sit on my lap. "But I'm naked," he said. "Hey," I said, "I'm the one who's going to be suffering. I was just looking for something easy. Would you rather run outside and touch the mailbox? The sun will be coming up in about 20 seconds. You want the whole neighborhood to see you?" "How long will I have to sit on you?" He asked. "I don't know, a minute or two. Maybe five, or seven."
<v David Sedaris>回想起来，我命令斯科特坐在我大腿上可能有点过分了。“但我没穿衣服，”他说。“嘿，”我说，“受苦的是我。我只是想找点简单的。你宁愿跑出去摸邮箱吗？太阳再过20秒就要出来了。你想让整个街区的人都看见你吗？”“我得坐在你身上多久？”他问。“我不知道，一两分钟。可能五或七分钟。”

00:21:41.070 --> 00:22:30.940
<v David Sedaris>I moved on to the easy chair and wearily patted my knee as if this were a great sacrifice. Scott slid into place, and I considered our reflection in the darkened TV screen. Here I was, one naked guy on my lap and three others ready to do my bidding. It was the stuff of dreams until I remembered that they were not doing these things of their own accord. This was not their pleasure but their punishment. And once it was over, they would make it a point to avoid me. Rumors would spread that I had slipped something into their Cokes, that I had tried to French Brad Clancy, that I had stolen $5 from Wade's pocket. Not even Mrs. Winters would wave at me. But all that would come later in a different life.
<v David Sedaris>我移到那把舒适的椅子上，疲惫地拍了拍膝盖，仿佛这是一个巨大的牺牲。斯科特坐了上来，我审视着我们在黑暗电视屏幕上的倒影。我就在这里，大腿上坐着一个裸体男人，另外三个准备听我差遣。这简直是梦想成真，直到我记得他们并非自愿做这些事。这不是他们的快乐，而是他们的惩罚。一旦结束，他们就会特意避开我。谣言会散布开来，说我在他们的可乐里下了药，说我试图强吻布拉德·克兰西，说我从韦德的口袋里偷了5美元。甚至温特斯太太也不会再对我挥手。但那一切都是后话，是在另一个生命中。

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:48.330
<v David Sedaris>For now, I would savor this poor imitation of tenderness, mapping Scott's shoulders, the small of his back, as he shuddered beneath my winning hand.
<v David Sedaris>现在，我要品味这可怜的温柔模仿，描绘着斯科特的肩膀，他的腰部，当他在我的胜券在握之下颤抖时。

