00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:28.120
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Playing Clinton in the Bush Leagues. And now the story of another precocious upstart, a winner in this case, someone who people love so much that he went into politics and took on some of the trappings of a minor, very minor, demagogue in the small Texas town of Gun Barrel City. Katy Vine tells this cautionary tale.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“在低级别联赛中扮演克林顿”。现在是另一个早熟的新贵的故事，在这个案例中他是个赢家，人们是如此爱他，以至于他投身政治，并在德克萨斯州枪管城这个小镇担任了某种微不足道、非常微不足道的煽动者的角色。凯蒂·范讲述了这个警示故事。

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:40.810
<v Katy Vine>Tye Thomas was one of those kids who appeared destined for success, the one adults look at and say, that kid's going places. The kind of kid who'd buy bulk candy and lottery tickets and hawk them in his classes at a markup of 300%, 400%, sometimes 500%.
<v Katy Vine>泰·托马斯是那种注定会成功的孩子，大人们看着他说，这孩子将来有出息。那种会大量购买糖果和彩票，然后以300%、400%、有时500%的加价在班级里倒卖的孩子。

00:23:40.810 --> 00:24:27.050
<v Tye Thomas>Now my best idea that I ever had was my dad used to carry me down to the local bar. It was called Captain John's in Gun Barrel City. They have in some Texas bars what they call a jukebox fund. And somebody will go around and collect $1 or $2 from everybody in the bar. And they go stick it in the jukebox. But what I would do is I would go around on a busy day, and collect $1 or $2 or $3 from everybody. And I was so young and cute then, people were just happy to give me the money. But what I'd do is I'd go put about $10 in the jukebox. And whatever was left over I'd stick in my pocket. That's just flat out stealing. However, I made a hell of a lot of money.
<v Tye Thomas>我最好的点子是，我爸爸经常带我去当地的一家酒吧。那家酒吧叫枪管城的约翰船长酒吧。德克萨斯的一些酒吧里有所谓的“点唱机基金”。有人会走一圈，从酒吧里的每个人那里收集1或2美元。然后他们把它投进点唱机里。但我会怎么做呢？我会在忙碌的日子里四处走动，从每个人那里收1、2或3美元。那时我很年轻可爱，人们很高兴把钱给我。但我会把大约10美元放进点唱机。剩下的钱我就揣进自己口袋。那根本就是偷窃。不过，我赚了相当多钱。

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:54.360
<v Katy Vine>The elements of most of Tye's stories are contained in this anecdote. He starts with a big idea. The big idea skirts the fine points of the law. Then he feels a little bit guilty about his actions. And he ignores it. When Tye was 15 years old, he founded a little six page newspaper, The Cedar Creek Briefs, which by the time he graduated, he had turned into a 24 page publication, mostly made up of ads. Before he went off to private college, he sold it for more money than his parents' combined income that year.
<v Katy Vine>泰的大多数故事都包含这些元素。他以一个宏大的想法开始。这个想法游走在法律的边缘。然后他会对自己的行为感到一点点内疚。然后他就无视了。泰15岁时，创办了一份六页的小报《雪松溪简报》，到他毕业时，他已经将其变成了一份24页的出版物，主要由广告组成。在他去私立大学之前，他卖掉它的钱比他父母当年的总收入还要多。

00:24:54.360 --> 00:25:20.130
<v Katy Vine>After college, he picked up where he left off. He bought the town newspaper, founded another one, and started buying up residential and commercial properties. Once he had his economic empire established, he started building his political empire. He decided to run for mayor. He launched a direct mail campaign. Every day after work, he went door to door asking people to vote for him. He even staffed a phone bank. For a town of 5,000, it was a well-oiled machine. Plus he was running as a reformer.
<v Katy Vine>大学毕业后，他重操旧业。他买了镇上的报纸，创办了另一份，并开始大量购买住宅和商业地产。一旦建立了他的经济帝国，他就开始建立他的政治帝国。他决定竞选市长。他发起了直接邮寄竞选活动。每天下班后，他挨家挨户请求人们投票给他。他甚至还配置了一个电话银行。对于一个5000人的小镇来说，这是一台运转良好的机器。况且他是以改革者的身份竞选的。

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:28.720
<v Mike Hannigan>When Tye Thomas came on the scene in Gun Barrel City as a political figure, Gun Barrel City was somewhat of a laughing stock.
<v Mike Hannigan>当泰·托马斯作为政治人物出现在枪管城时，枪管城在某种程度上是个笑话。

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:34.290
<v Katy Vine>Mike Hannigan is the editor of the Cedar Creek Pilot , which had been the main competing newspaper to Tye's two newspapers.
<v Katy Vine>迈克·汉尼根是《雪松溪领航员》的编辑，该报一直是泰两份报纸的主要竞争对手。

00:25:34.290 --> 00:26:25.580
<v Mike Hannigan>We had a city manager that one time came out and said someone had put a bomb under his car. And it ended up being a jug of gasoline. And this is a town that was coming off of watching our mayor kick in a door in City Hall on TV. And Tye Thomas looked like the kind of guy that would make that kind of stuff go away. He knew how to sell things. He knew how to sell himself. People believed that he would be able to sell Gun Barrel City. I mean he was a young guy, new ideas, good personality, had a great smile. Everybody liked him. How could this kid get us into trouble?
<v Mike Hannigan>我们有位城市经理曾有一次跳出来说有人在他车下放了炸弹。最后发现那只是一个汽油罐。这就是一个刚看着我们的市长在电视上踢开市政厅大门的镇子。而泰·托马斯看起来像是那种能让这些破事消失的人。他知道如何推销东西。他知道如何推销自己。人们相信他有能力推销枪管城。我的意思是，他是个年轻人，有新点子，性格好，笑容灿烂。每个人都喜欢他。这孩子怎么会让我们惹上麻烦？

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:34.170
<v Katy Vine>On election day, Tye went down to City Hall, where the secretary had posted the results. He had won 67% of the vote, a landslide. He was 21 years old.
<v Katy Vine>选举日那天，泰去了市政厅，秘书已经公布了结果。他赢得了67%的选票，大获全胜。他21岁。

00:26:34.170 --> 00:27:14.980
<v Tye Thomas>You know after three months of working for something, I felt like doing cartwheels in the parking lot. I felt like taking my clothes off and running down the highway naked. But I contained myself. I remember I wrote a journal entry the next morning, that next Sunday morning, May 7, And it felt so weird waking up on the couch-- because I sleep on the couch every night-- it felt so weird waking up on the couch knowing that I was now the mayor of my city.
<v Tye Thomas>你知道，为一件事工作了三个月后，我觉得自己想在停车场翻跟头。我觉得自己想脱掉衣服在高速公路上裸奔。但我克制住了自己。我记得那天第二天早上，也就是5月7日那个星期天早上，我写了日记。在那沙发上醒来感觉很奇怪——因为我每晚都睡在沙发上——在那沙发上醒来知道自己现在是这个城市的市长，感觉真奇怪。

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:27.250
<v Katy Vine>A lot of people can win an election. But to win an election in a landslide usually takes a certain kind of politician, one who motivates the community and one who also feeds off the affirmation of his people. Tye Thomas is one of those politicians.
<v Katy Vine>很多人都能赢得选举。但要以压倒性优势赢得选举，通常需要某种特定的政客，一个能够激励社区，同时也从人们的肯定中汲取力量的人。泰·托马斯就是那种政客。

00:27:27.250 --> 00:28:19.480
<v Tye Thomas>The first three or four months after I was elected were I would have to say probably the greatest four months of my life, looking back. I was invited often to speak to groups to share my vision for the city. I received lots of positive press, lots of great publicity. The city was receiving wonderful publicity as a result of my winning the election. I was working on the promise that I would return the peace and the serenity to City Hall, which I didn't do. I actually probably made it worse.
<v Tye Thomas>回头来看，我当选后的头三四个月可能是我一生中最美好的四个月。我经常被邀请去向团体发表演讲，分享我对城市的愿景。我收到了很多积极的媒体报道，很多很棒的宣传。因为我的当选，这个城市获得了美妙的宣传。我致力于实现我将把和平与宁静带回市政厅的承诺，但我没有做到。我实际上可能把事情弄得更糟了。

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:36.120
<v Katy Vine>One of the things that makes Tye such a remarkable character is that he's so candid. His candor was refreshing, maybe even necessary, for success in a town whose motto was "we shoot you straight." But in politics, like in all sorts of careers, there are just some times you are better off keeping your mouth shut. Again, editor Mike Hannigan.
<v Katy Vine>让泰成为如此杰出人物的原因之一是他非常坦率。他的坦率令人耳目一新，甚至可能是在一个座右铭是“我们直言不讳”的镇子上成功所必需的。但在政治中，就像在各种职业中一样，有些时候你最好还是闭上嘴。再次引用编辑迈克·汉尼根的话。

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:57.540
<v Mike Hannigan>I remember the time he went on a Dallas radio station for a win a date with the mayor contest, where they actually had women calling in and talking to Tye and Tye talking to them. And one of them got to have a date with Tye Thomas.
<v Mike Hannigan>我记得他曾去达拉斯的一家广播电台参加“赢取与市长约会”的比赛，那里真的有女性打来电话和泰交谈，泰也和她们谈话。其中一位真的和泰·托马斯约会了。

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:58.890
<v Katy Vine>So somebody did win a date?
<v Katy Vine>所以真的有人赢得了约会？

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:00.540
<v Mike Hannigan>Oh, yeah.
<v Mike Hannigan>哦，是的。

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:04.050
<v Katy Vine>Were people in the community embarrassed, though, that he did that?
<v Katy Vine>但社区里的人会因为他那样做而感到尴尬吗？

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:47.010
<v Mike Hannigan>People in the community were horrified. One of the best examples was he goes to a bar and admittedly-- in a newspaper article admits that he drank a little too much and didn't think he should be driving home. So the first thing he thought of was to call the police to give him a hand, call for a police escort home. And all this is not quite what the average person would think that the police was supposed to be doing. But Tye never gave it a second thought. I mean hey, he was in trouble. The police were there to serve. I'm Tye Thomas. Let me give them a call.
<v Mike Hannigan>社区里的人感到震惊。最好的例子之一就是他去酒吧，而且承认——在报纸文章中承认他喝多了，觉得不应该开车回家。所以他想到的第一件事就是打警察局寻求帮助，叫警车护送回家。而这一切并不完全是普通人认为警察应该做的事。但泰从来没想过第二次。我的意思是，嘿，他遇到了麻烦。警察就是为民服务的。我是泰·托马斯。让我给他们打个电话。

00:29:47.010 --> 00:30:26.340
<v Katy Vine>There is more than one story like this about Tye stretching the boundaries of what was acceptable. They eventually led to a DA investigation. And on April 27, 2001, Tye was indicted by a grand jury on perjury charges, a Class A misdemeanor. He was accused of lying about his residency in the town on his application for mayor. Remember, Tye was the town hero. He had won in a landslide, which is maybe why he assumed the public would give him the benefit of the doubt. And so Tye Thomas did what a lot of popular politicians accused of wrongdoing have done before him. He denied the charges. He said the attack was politically motivated. Tye says his problems started right after he declared publicly that he wanted to run for state rep against an incumbent named Clyde Alexander.
<v Katy Vine>关于泰挑战可接受底线的故事不止这一个。它们最终导致了地区检察官的调查。2001年4月27日，泰被大陪审团以伪证罪起诉，这是一项A级轻罪。他被指控在申请市长职位时谎报在镇上的居住情况。记住，泰是镇上的英雄。他以压倒性优势获胜，也许这就是为什么他认为公众会给他宽容。于是泰·托马斯做了很多被指控有不当行为的受欢迎政客以前做过的事。他否认了指控。他说这次攻击是政治动机。泰说他的麻烦始于他公开宣布想竞选州代表，挑战现任者克莱德·亚历山大之后。

00:30:26.340 --> 00:31:00.250
<v Tye Thomas>That's when all of my troubles began. That's when his ally, Democrat District Attorney Donna Bennett opened up all of her investigations into me and things I had done in the past. She used investigations as a political war tool to discredit my character and my job that I was doing as mayor.
<v Tye Thomas>那就是我所有麻烦开始的时候。那就是他的盟友，民主党地区检察官唐娜·贝内特对我以及我过去所做的事展开所有调查的时候。她利用调查作为政治战工具来抹黑我的人格，以及我作为市长所做的工作。

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:09.500
<v Katy Vine>There is, of course, one problem with this conspiracy theory. And that is that the incumbent, Clyde Alexander, didn't run. In any case, it shouldn't have mattered. Mike Hannigan.
<v Katy Vine>当然，这个阴谋论有一个问题。那就是现任者克莱德·亚历山大根本没参选。无论如何，这都不重要。迈克·汉尼根。

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:20.840
<v Mike Hannigan>Now I think Tye probably could have survived all of that. But that's when he went to the alcohol, and the drugs, and the Xanax.
<v Mike Hannigan>现在我认为泰本来可能从这一切中幸存下来的。但那就是他开始酗酒、吸毒和服用赞安诺的时候。

00:31:20.840 --> 00:32:00.720
<v Tye Thomas>It was a Monday morning. The grand jury was the upcoming Thursday. Even though I knew beyond a shadow of a doubt that I was not guilty of the crimes that she was trying to present against me, there was this little voice in my head that's saying, Golly, four to 20 years in prison? I'd rather die. I was scared. I've got to admit to you, Monday morning I woke up at about 7 o'clock and I was scared to death. And I took two, or three, or four Xanax. I can't remember how many. Xanax is an anti-anxiety medication that my doctor prescribes to me.
<v Tye Thomas>那是一个星期一的早上。大陪审团的会议在那个星期四。尽管我毫无疑问地知道我并没有犯下她试图向我指控的那些罪行，但我脑子里有一个小声音说，天哪，四到20年的牢狱之灾？我宁愿死。我很害怕。我必须向你承认，星期一早上我大约7点醒来，我被吓死了。我吃了两片、三片或四片赞安诺。我不记得吃了多少了。赞安诺是我医生给我开的一种抗焦虑药物。

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:56.170
<v Tye Thomas>And then that evening I went out and I started drinking vodka tonics. And by the end of the evening, I was drinking straight vodka. And I was completely and totally intoxicated. Well I don't know when, but sometime later I vomited, because I had had so much to drink. And I was still asleep on the couch. My ex-fiancee [? Kathy ?] decides to come over. She finds me passed out on the couch in a pool of vomit. She picks up the phone and calls 911, and tells the dispatcher that she was in the mayor's condo and that he had overdosed on Xanax.
<v Tye Thomas>那天晚上我出去开始喝伏特加汤力。到了晚上结束时，我在喝纯伏特加。我完全、彻底地醉了。嗯，我不知道是什么时候，但后来我呕吐了，因为我喝得太多了。我仍然在沙发上睡着。我的前未婚妻凯西决定过来。她发现我醉倒在沙发上，躺在呕吐物中。她拿起电话打了911，告诉接线员她在市长的公寓里，他服用赞安诺过量了。

00:32:56.170 --> 00:33:26.640
<v Tye Thomas>I didn't wake up until there were about six police officers in my house and about eight paramedics. She'd made a 911 call, which is public record. And I knew that the newspaper would pick up on that. And boy it would be the hot story that week. So in some nutso, idiot, ridiculous, drunken state of mind, I decided I wanted to go to jail.
<v Tye Thomas>直到屋里有大约六名警察和八名医护人员，我才醒来。她打了911报警电话，这是公共记录。我知道报纸会报道这件事。天哪，那会是那一周的热门新闻。所以在某种疯狂、愚蠢、荒唐、醉酒的状态下，我决定我想坐牢。

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:28.550
<v Officer Red>Gun Barrel Police Department.
<v Officer Red>枪管城警察局。

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:29.990
<v Tye Thomas>Who's speaking, Red?
<v Tye Thomas>谁在说话，雷德？

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:30.950
<v Officer Red>This is Red.
<v Officer Red>我是雷德。

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:33.510
<v Tye Thomas>Red, this is Mayor Thomas.
<v Tye Thomas>雷德，我是市长托马斯。

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:34.990
<v Kathy>Get ?] off the phone.
<v Kathy>滚蛋。

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:39.810
<v Tye Thomas>I need an officer to come and immediately arrest me.
<v Tye Thomas>我需要一名警官立刻过来逮捕我。

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:42.610
<v Officer Red>What's the emergency?
<v Officer Red>什么紧急情况？

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:50.110
<v Tye Thomas>I am guilty of public intoxication. And I need to be taken to jail.
<v Tye Thomas>我犯了公共场合醉酒罪。我需要被带进监狱。

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:53.900
<v Officer Red>Well, you can't be publicly intoxicated in your own home, sir.
<v Officer Red>嗯，先生，你在自己家里不能算在公共场合醉酒。

00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:57.150
<v Tye Thomas>Well, I'll be in the parking lot.
<v Tye Thomas>好吧，我会去停车场的。

00:33:57.150 --> 00:34:01.410
<v Officer Red>Mr. Mayor, as soon as I got one available, I'll send him right away.
<v Officer Red>市长先生，我一有人手就会立刻派人过去。

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:25.840
<v Tye Thomas>And I think I called five or six times. I didn't know what I said until my competitor newspaper got a copy of all the 911 tapes, and so did the TV stations in Dallas, and played them. It was pretty embarrassing to hear yourself drunk on the 10 o'clock news.
<v Tye Thomas>我想我打了五六次。我不知道自己说了什么，直到我的竞争对手报纸拿到了所有911录音的副本，达拉斯的电视台也拿到了，并播放了它们。在10点新闻里听到自己醉酒的样子真是太尴尬了。

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:44.670
<v Katy Vine>These days, having attended his share of AA meetings, Tye Thomas will be the first to admit that he had problems with substance abuse. But you get the sense that he first grasped the seriousness of his trouble just as it was being broadcast to everyone in the Dallas-Forth Worth area. The incident was the nail in the coffin. 21 days later, he resigned as mayor.
<v Katy Vine>这些天，参加过几次戒酒互助会会议后，泰·托马斯会第一个承认他有药物滥用问题。但你会觉得他是在麻烦事被向整个达拉斯-沃斯堡地区广播时，才第一次抓住了严重性。这件事是棺材上的最后一颗钉子。21天后，他辞去了市长职务。

00:34:44.670 --> 00:35:08.050
<v Katy Vine>Afterwards, he had bouts of depression. He sat in his condo with the blinds closed for days at a time, not bathing, wearing only his bathrobe. He became paranoid, and went so far as to re-shred paper shreddings in his apartment. He thought he was being watched. Eventually he left town.
<v Katy Vine>之后，他陷入了抑郁。他一连几天拉着百叶窗坐在公寓里，不洗澡，只穿着浴袍。他变得多疑，甚至去重新粉碎公寓里的纸屑。他以为自己被监视了。最终他离开了小镇。

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:41.560
<v Katy Vine>One of the most striking things about the whole episode is the extremes of Tye's confidence and his fragility. It seems strange. How can somebody who had such absolute faith in his ability to lead be dragged into a drug and alcohol spiral by charges he could have beaten? But at least in Tye's case, the confidence and fragility seem linked. They both come from a failure to anticipate the thought that occurs to all the rest of us as second nature. What if I fail? What if I can't lead? What if they don't like me? It's a very innocent thought. Tye had an innocence about other people's faith in him and an innocence about his faith in himself.
<v Katy Vine>整个事件中最引人注目的是泰的自信与脆弱之间的极端对比。这看起来很奇怪。一个对领导能力有绝对信心的人怎么会被他本来可以打赢的指控拖进毒品和酒精的漩涡呢？但至少在泰的案例中，自信和脆弱似乎是联系在一起的。它们都源于未能预见那个对我们其他人来说是第二天性会想到的念头。如果我失败了怎么办？如果我不能领导怎么办？如果他们不喜欢我怎么办？那是一个非常天真的想法。泰对于别人对他的信仰有一种天真，对他自己对自己的信仰也有种天真。

00:35:41.560 --> 00:36:10.740
<v Tye Thomas>You see when I ran for mayor, and after I was first elected, I really felt that emotionally and mentally I was invincible. I didn't believe that anything or anybody could change my self-esteem or the way that I felt about myself. And I was dead wrong.
<v Tye Thomas>你看，当我竞选市长时，以及我第一次当选后，我真的觉得在情感上和精神上我是不可战胜的。我不相信任何事或任何人能改变我的自尊或我对自己的感觉。但我大错特错了。

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:17.260
<v Katy Vine>If you could maybe wipe the town memory absolutely clean, would you go back and try again?
<v Katy Vine>如果你能把这个镇的记忆彻底抹掉，你会回去再试一次吗？

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:17.860
<v Tye Thomas>No.
<v Tye Thomas>不会。

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:22.160
<v Katy Vine>If they had no memory of who Tye Thomas was and you were going in for the first time?
<v Katy Vine>如果他们完全不记得泰·托马斯是谁，而你是第一次进去？

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:24.320
<v Tye Thomas>No, I wouldn't go back.
<v Tye Thomas>不会，我不会回去。

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:25.780
<v Katy Vine>How come?
<v Katy Vine>为什么？

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:51.990
<v Tye Thomas>I've become very sensitive to how situations that I place myself in are going to affect me emotionally. And that's not something I would ever put myself through again. Even if it had a completely different outcome, even if I knew beyond a shadow of a doubt that it was going to be positive, I wouldn't go back and do it.
<v Tye Thomas>我对自身所处的环境如何影响我的情绪变得非常敏感。那不是我会再让自己经历一次的事情。即使结果完全不同，即使我毫无疑问地知道它会是积极的，我也不会回去做这件事。

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:57.100
<v Katy Vine>Despite all that, as Mike Hannigan points out, his term wasn't exactly a failure.
<v Katy Vine>尽管如此，正如迈克·汉尼根指出的那样，他的任期并不完全是个失败。

00:36:57.100 --> 00:37:10.300
<v Mike Hannigan>He made himself into exactly what he wanted to make himself into. He became a public figure, almost an icon.
<v Mike Hannigan>他把自己塑造成了他想塑造的样子。他成为了一个公众人物，几乎是一个偶像。

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:27.030
<v Tye Thomas>Light a fire within you. Some will come and join you. And others will come just to watch you burn. And back then, I had a strong fire going with me. And people couldn't get enough of it.
<v Tye Thomas>点燃你心中的火焰。有些人会加入你。有些人则会仅仅为了看着你燃烧而来。那时，我心中燃着熊熊烈火。人们对此如痴如醉。

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:48.510
<v Mike Hannigan>Tye Thomas has an unholy hold on the Gun Barrel City imagination. He has been gone for years now, and I still get phone calls with people telling me, hey, he just did this. Hey, he just did that.
<v Mike Hannigan>泰·托马斯在枪管城的想象中占有一种不圣的统治力。他已经离开多年了，但我仍然会接到电话，有人告诉我，嘿，他刚才做了这个。嘿，他刚才做了那个。

00:37:48.510 --> 00:38:08.900
<v Tye Thomas>Let me tell you, it's a wonderful feeling to go to the newsstand and buy a newspaper and not have to worry about your picture or name being on the front page. At first I loved it. And now that's something that I grew to despise.
<v Tye Thomas>让我告诉你，去报刊亭买份报纸，不用担心自己的照片或名字出现在头版上，那是一种美妙的感觉。起初我很喜欢它，而现在那是我开始鄙视的事情。

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:36.160
<v Katy Vine>Some politicians take a lifetime to get to the point Tye got to in a year. The stunning rise to the top, the scandal, and the fall from grace. Tye says he hasn't entirely ruled out a future run for office. But he figures he'll have to wait at least 15 years before he can claim it's all behind him, that he's someone else. So he waits. He's not even 25.
<v Katy Vine>有些政客花了一辈子才达到泰在一年内达到的地步。令人惊叹的崛起，丑闻，以及失宠后的跌落。泰说他并没有完全排除未来竞选公职的可能。但他觉得他至少得等15年，才能声称这一切都已经过去了，他已经变成了另一个人。所以他等待着。他甚至还不到25岁。

00:38:36.160 --> 00:39:57.710
<v Ira Glass>Katy Vine is a writer for the magazine Texas Monthly . Coming up, US officials, many of them young and charming, come into Iraq carrying their message of hope, and democracy, and freedom. And why even the Iraqis who want to hear that message don't seem to. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>凯蒂·范是《德克萨斯月刊》杂志的撰稿人。接下来，美国官员，许多人年轻而迷人，带着关于希望、民主和自由的信息来到伊拉克。为什么连那些想听到这些信息的伊拉克人似乎都听不进去。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:39:57.710 --> 00:40:16.000
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, I'm in Charge Here, stories of people who get filled with American entrepreneurial spirit to take over, create something new, full of self confidence, and what sometimes happens to them.
<v Ira Glass>这是《这就是美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种关于该主题的报道。今天的节目，“这里我做主”，讲述的是那些充满美国创业精神，接手并创造新事物，充满自信的人，以及有时发生在他们身上的事。

00:40:16.000 --> 00:41:00.190
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, You Talkin' to Me? Nearly every day, there's some story from Iraq about how badly the US occupation there is going. Soldiers getting shot, basic services still not working. And one of the big problems there is the information gap between the Iraqis and the Americans who are running the country. One small, telling sign of that gap, even the name of the agency running the country hasn't gotten out to most Iraqis. That agency's name? It's called the Coalition Provisional Authority, or CPA. When Iraqis know any name, it's usually the name the agency used to be called, ORHA, the Office of Reconstruction and Humanitarian Assistance. Well, reporter Adam Davidson says the information gap is one of the most ominous things about living in Baghdad these days.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经进入节目的第三幕。第三幕，“你在跟我说话吗？”几乎每天，都有关于伊拉克美军占领进展得多么糟糕的新闻。士兵中枪，基本服务依然无法运作。而那里的一大问题是伊拉克人和管理这个国家的美国人之间的信息鸿沟。这个鸿沟的一个小小的、显著的迹象是，连管理这个国家的机构名称都没有传达到大多数伊拉克人那里。那个机构的名字？它叫“联盟临时管理局”，简称CPA。当伊拉克人知道名字时，通常是该机构以前的名字，ORHA，“重建和人道主义援助办公室”。嗯，记者亚当·戴维森说，信息鸿沟是这些天在巴格达生活最令人担忧的事情之一。

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:23.580
<v Adam Davidson>There's an information void. And as a result, rumors are everywhere. Amjad, my translator, tells me the new ones every morning. And then I hear them repeated by different people all day. People say the Americans are burning Iraqi currency in big trash barrels at gas stations, because they want Iraqis to be poor. They say Israelis are buying up the most valuable real estate in Iraq so they can occupy Iraq just like Palestine.
<v Adam Davidson>存在一个信息真空。结果，谣言无处不在。我的翻译阿姆贾德每天早上都告诉我新的谣言。然后我整天听到不同的人重复这些谣言。人们说美国人在加油站的垃圾桶里烧掉伊拉克货币，因为他们想让伊拉克人变穷。他们说以色列人正在购买伊拉克最有价值的房地产，这样他们就能像巴勒斯坦一样占领伊拉克。

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:42.590
<v Adam Davidson>Then there's the rumor you hear most often, that Saddam Hussein is living in the US somewhere in a big mansion. I hear this all the time, that Saddam worked with the Americans, arranged for the American invasion so that the US could take over the country and steal its oil. In the last few weeks this rumor has changed a bit. People now say Saddam is living in the White House.
<v Adam Davidson>然后是那种你听到最频繁的谣言，即萨达姆·侯赛因住在美国某处的豪宅里。我一直听到这个，说萨达姆与美国人合作，安排了美国入侵，以便美国可以接管国家并窃取石油。在过去几周，这个谣言稍微改变了一点。人们现在说萨达姆住在白宫里。

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:45.460
<v Mustafa>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Mustafa>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:59.440
<v Translator>Whether the rumor is right or correct or not, what we do is convey the rumor by itself without giving our own point of view or our own opinion about this rumor.
<v Translator>不管谣言是对是错，我们所做的只是传达谣言本身，而不给出我们自己的观点或对这个谣言的评价。

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:19.650
<v Adam Davidson>Mustafa publishes Al-Saah, The Clock, one of the newspapers that have sprung up in Baghdad since the fall of Saddam Hussein. So many papers have appeared that nobody knows exactly how many there are. The last count I heard was 105. Mustafa tells me the paper has 30 reporters. Every day they try to find some American somewhere who will answer their questions.
<v Adam Davidson>穆斯塔法出版了《时钟》，这是萨达姆·侯赛因倒台后在巴格达涌现的报纸之一。出现了这么多报纸，没人确切知道有多少。我听到的最新统计是105份。穆斯塔法告诉我报社有30名记者。他们每天都在尝试在某处找到一个愿意回答他们问题的美国人。

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:23.150
<v Mustafa>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Mustafa>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:33.550
<v Translator>We need to interview members of ORHA personally to ask them their questions, to know their feelings, to know what kind of measures they are taking.
<v Translator>我们需要亲自采访ORHA的成员来询问他们问题，了解他们的感受，了解他们正在采取什么样的措施。

00:42:33.550 --> 00:42:35.350
<v Mustafa>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Mustafa>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:46.000
<v Translator>We always try to get information from ORHA. We are always sending our reporters there to the office. But they found difficulties in getting this information.
<v Translator>我们总是试图从ORHA获取信息。我们总是派记者去他们的办公室。但他们发现获取这些信息很困难。

00:42:46.000 --> 00:43:14.680
<v Adam Davidson>So they're left printing rumors. One issue of Al-Saah reported that American soldiers were giving pornography to schoolgirls, another rumor I heard a lot. Another explained how the Americans had promised to give $50 to every Iraqi. The photo on the front page was of an American soldier attacking an older man. In red writing along the sides there was a prayer, "Merciful God, deliver us from this damnation." All this was an Arabic, along with editorials denouncing the US occupation.
<v Adam Davidson>所以他们只能印刷谣言。《时钟》的一期报道说美国士兵在给女学生发放色情制品，这是我听到的另一个谣言。另一篇解释了美国人是如何承诺给每个伊拉克人50美元的。头版的照片是一名美国士兵在攻击一位老人。侧面用红色文字写着祈祷，“仁慈的上帝，从这诅咒中解救我们吧。”所有这些都是阿拉伯语，还有谴责美国占领的社论。

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:45.380
<v Adam Davidson>But like a lot of Iraqi newspapers, one of Al-Saah's eight pages is in English. The English page seems to come from some other newspaper. It's completely pro-American. The photo on that page was of a friendly female soldier giving a bottle of water to a grateful young Iraqi girl. There's an article about how the Americans had helped to open a school. As one reporter told me, the English page is for the Americans to read, so they won't get mad at Al-Saah. The Arabic pages are for the Iraqis.
<v Adam Davidson>但像许多伊拉克报纸一样，《时钟》八页中的一页是英语。英文版看起来像是来自其他报纸。它完全是亲美的。那一页的照片是一名友好的女兵给一个感激的年轻伊拉克女孩送去一瓶水。有一篇关于美国人如何帮助开设学校的文章。正如一位记者告诉我的，英语版是给美国人看的，这样他们就不会对《时钟》生气。阿拉伯语版是给伊拉克人看的。

00:43:45.380 --> 00:44:01.310
<v Adam Davidson>In general, Iraqis know the Americans are in charge. But they have no idea what the Americans here are up to. Most people have heard of Paul Bremer, the guy who's in charge of Iraq right now. But they don't know anything about him. These men were standing near the [? Jamuhuri ?] Bridge, about a block from the American headquarters in Iraq.
<v Adam Davidson>总的来说，伊拉克人知道美国人在掌管一切。但他们不知道这里的美国人在干什么。大多数人都听说过保罗·布雷默，现在掌管伊拉克的人。但他们对他一无所知。这些人在距离伊拉克美军总部约一个街区的贾姆胡里大桥附近站着。

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:04.830
<v Adam Davidson>What have you heard from the Americans about what their plans are?
<v Adam Davidson>你们从美国人那里听到了关于他们有什么计划的消息吗？

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:08.550
<v Iraqi Man>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Man>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:44:08.550 --> 00:44:10.840
<v Translator>I heard nothing. I heard nothing from them.
<v Translator>我什么都没听到。我没从他们那里听到任何消息。

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:13.250
<v Adam Davidson>What do you know about Bremer? What do you know about Bremer?
<v Adam Davidson>关于布雷默你们知道些什么？关于布雷默你们知道些什么？

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:15.030
<v Iraqi Man>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Man>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:18.980
<v Translator>I don't anything. If he--
<v Translator>我不知道任何事。如果他——

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:20.950
<v Iraqi Man>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Man>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:24.710
<v Translator>Only promises. Only promises. That's all I know about Bremer.
<v Translator>只有承诺。只有承诺。关于布雷默我只知道这些。

00:44:24.710 --> 00:44:26.900
<v Adam Davidson>If he were walking down the street, would you recognize him?
<v Adam Davidson>如果他走在街上，你们认得出他吗？

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:28.620
<v Translator>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Translator>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:35.690
<v Iraqi Man>No. No. [SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Man>不。不。[阿拉伯语发言]

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:40.810
<v Translator>If he was walking along we don't know him. Maybe you are Bremer.
<v Translator>如果他走过来我们不知道他。也许你就是布雷默。

00:44:40.810 --> 00:45:13.540
<v Adam Davidson>Talking to people in Baghdad, I've thought a lot about what it would be like to be back home in America if the situation were reversed. If there were this group of Iraqis who don't speak English running my country, knowing they're making plans that will affect every part of my life and not knowing anything about what those plans are. Not even knowing anything about who those people are. One thing you hear all the time is if the Americans wanted to fix all this with all their money and power, they could. So the only explanation is they don't care. Or worse, they want Iraqis to suffer.
<v Adam Davidson>在巴格达与人交谈时，我经常想到如果情况反过来，回到美国会是什么样子。如果有一群不会说英语的伊拉克人运行我的国家，知道他们在制定会影响我生活方方面面的计划，却对这些计划一无所知。甚至对这些人是谁一无所知。有一点你经常听到，那就是如果美国人想用他们所有的钱和权力解决这一切，他们是可以做到的。所以唯一的解释是他们不在乎。或者更糟，他们想让伊拉克人受苦。

00:45:13.540 --> 00:45:44.300
<v Adam Davidson>The headquarters of the US administration in Iraq is right in the center of Baghdad, Saddam's Republican Palace. It's like a fortress. All you can see from the street is barbed concertina wire and a whole lot of US infantry. In the distance is this large arch and, through it, a tree-lined road. It's like a small city in there. This was Saddam's main governmental complex, a huge area, big enough that the US has set up bus routes inside with dozens of buildings and thousands of Americans living and working there.
<v Adam Davidson>美军在伊拉克的管理总部就在巴格达市中心，萨达姆的共和国宫。它就像一座堡垒。从街上你只能看到铁丝网和大量的美国步兵。远处是这个巨大的拱门，通过它，是一条林荫道。那里面就像一个小城市。这是萨达姆的主要政府大楼，一个巨大的区域，大到美国人在里面建立了公交线路，有几十栋大楼，成千上万的美国人在那里生活和工作。

00:45:44.300 --> 00:46:03.740
<v Adam Davidson>And this gate is like the border between the country of Iraq and the American country within a country. It's where US soldiers shot and killed protesters this week who were throwing stones. Every day there's a crowd of Iraqis at the entrance off to one side of all that barbed wire. They're trying to get in there, hoping to talk to someone in charge about whatever problem they're having.
<v Adam Davidson>而这个大门就像伊拉克国家和美国内部国家之间的边界。这是本周美军士兵射杀投掷石块的抗议者的地方。每天都有大群伊拉克人在所有铁丝网的一侧入口处。他们试图进去，希望能和负责的人谈谈他们遇到的任何问题。

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:06.950
<v Iraqi Women>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Women>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:34.260
<v Adam Davidson>Two women are there dressed in their best clothes, like they were going to a job interview. They're principals, they say, and they want to talk to whichever American is in charge of the Ministry of Education. In all the postwar confusion, some guy no one knew just showed up one day, and said he's now in charge of their school district. He's awful, they say, bossing everyone around and stealing money. They want a different guy to be placed in charge. Someone prepared a letter for them, which neither can read because they don't know English.
<v Adam Davidson>两名女性穿着她们最好的衣服在那里，就像要去参加工作面试一样。她们说她们是校长，想和负责教育部的美国人谈谈。在战后所有的混乱中，一个没人认识的家伙某天突然出现，说他现在负责他们的学区。她们说他很糟糕，对每个人颐指气使，还偷钱。她们想换个负责的人。有人为她们准备了一封信，但两人都读不懂，因为她们不懂英语。

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:37.380
<v Soldier>Hey, tell these people to back up.
<v Soldier>嘿，告诉这些人后退。

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:52.500
<v Adam Davidson>A soldier comes up and tells them to get back from the gate. They don't know what he's saying, and he doesn't know what they're saying. So they keep standing there and eventually the soldier walks away. It turns out this is the wrong gate if you want to talk to an American.
<v Adam Davidson>一名士兵走过来叫她们离大门远点。她们不知道他在说什么，他也不知道她们在说什么。所以她们继续站在那里，最终士兵走开了。原来如果你想和美国人谈话，这里走错了门。

00:46:52.500 --> 00:47:24.270
<v Adam Davidson>There is a right gate. It's off to the side behind a bunch of trees where army specialist Laura Moore, a 21 year old in the Civil Affairs Corps, is standing behind some barbed concertina wire. She has blue eyes and blond hair that comes down out of her bulletproof helmet. You can just picture her walking around a mall back home with her friends. And here she is, the face of the US occupation of Iraq. In the whole country, only Laura Moore and a small number of co-workers have the job of meeting with average Iraqis, finding out what's bothering them and trying to offer solutions.
<v Adam Davidson>有个正确的门。它在树林后面的一侧，美国民政部门21岁的士兵劳拉·摩尔正站在铁丝网后面。她有着蓝眼睛和金色头发，从防弹头盔下露出来。你可以想象她在家乡和朋友们在商场散步的样子。而在这里，她是美军占领伊拉克的面孔。在整个国家，只有劳拉·摩尔和少数同事有任务与普通伊拉克人会面，找出困扰他们的问题并试图提供解决方案。

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:53.090
<v Adam Davidson>Even though she could move into the shade of the trees, she stands with a translator out in the hot sun. A few yards away, there is a long line of Iraqis who are also standing in the sun. Most of them are older men, and they look sick like they're wilting in the 115 degree heat. When they are waved over, they walk up to the barbed wire and tell their problems to Moore's not very good translator, who does his best in a kind of broken English to tell Moore what they're saying. Then he translates her response. It's all sorts of things. People want money.
<v Adam Davidson>尽管她可以移到树荫下，但她和翻译站在炎炎烈日下。几码远的地方，是一长排也站在太阳底下的伊拉克人。他们大多数是上了年纪的男人，看起来病恹恹的，好像在115华氏度的高温下枯萎了一样。当被招手示意过来时，他们走到铁丝网旁，向摩尔那个不怎么样的翻译诉说他们的问题，翻译尽其所能地用蹩脚的英语告诉摩尔他们在说什么。然后他翻译她的回应。各种各样的问题。人们想要钱。

00:47:53.090 --> 00:47:55.820
<v Iraqi  Man 1>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi  Man 1>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:59.690
<v Translator>He has a car which was looted one month ago.
<v Translator>他的车在一个月前被抢走了。

00:47:59.690 --> 00:48:03.920
<v Laura Moore>OK, he's going to have to take his civil complaint to the police academy.
<v Laura Moore>好吧，他必须把他的民事诉讼带到警察学院去。

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:11.790
<v Translator>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Translator>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:16.290
<v Iraqi Man 1>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Iraqi Man 1>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:48:16.290 --> 00:48:40.770
<v Laura Moore>Unfortunately, there's really nothing that we can do for you right here. If the Police Academy is working your situation, hopefully they'll find your car soon, OK? Now for temporary aid, I'm going to advise you to go to the Red Crescent in Mansour, because hopefully they'll be able to help you out financially, something like that. Maybe help you get your feet back on the ground and get a new car, OK?
<v Laura Moore>不幸的是，我们在这里真的无能为力。如果警察学院正在处理你的情况，希望他们很快能找到你的车，好吗？现在为了临时援助，我建议你去曼苏尔的红新月会，因为希望他们能在经济上帮助你，之类的。也许能帮助你重新站稳脚跟，买辆新车，好吗？

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:55.420
<v Adam Davidson>Moore says this is what she does pretty much every time. The Iraqis talk to her, and then she tells them she can't help them and tells them to go away. She says nobody gets past the gate. Nobody gets inside the palace to talk to whoever will decide their case. It's for safety reasons.
<v Adam Davidson>摩尔说她几乎每次都这么做。伊拉克人跟她说话，然后她告诉他们她帮不了他们，并让他们离开。她说没人能过得了大门。没人能进去宫殿和决定他们案件的人谈话。这是出于安全原因。

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:59.200
<v Adam Davidson>Is it hard sending people away like that?
<v Adam Davidson>那样赶走人很难受吗？

00:48:59.200 --> 00:49:07.980
<v Laura Moore>You get worse cases than that. And I see about anywhere between 85 and 100 people a day. So--
<v Laura Moore>你看到的案件比那更糟糕。而且我每天大约看85到100人。所以——

00:49:07.980 --> 00:49:09.230
<v Adam Davidson>You're getting used to it.
<v Adam Davidson>你正在习惯它。

00:49:09.230 --> 00:49:11.820
<v Laura Moore>You get used to it.
<v Laura Moore>你会习惯的。

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:35.610
<v Adam Davidson>A few times a day, someone has a complaint that the Army can actually do something about, like their car or their house was destroyed by the US military. With those people, Moore takes their information, and says someone will look it over, make a decision and get back to them in two months. She always says two months. Of course, there are no working phones in most parts of Baghdad. And there is no postal service. So it's not clear how she'll get back to anybody.
<v Adam Davidson>每天有几次，有人有美军实际上可以做点什么的投诉，比如他们的车或房子被美军摧毁了。对于那些人，摩尔记录他们的信息，并说会有人查看，做决定并在两个月内回复他们。她总是说两个月。当然，巴格达大部分地区没有可用的电话。而且没有邮政服务。所以不清楚她将如何回复任何人。

00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:37.600
<v Adam Davidson>Are you learning any Arabic?
<v Adam Davidson>你在学阿拉伯语吗？

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:44.850
<v Laura Moore>A little. Just some basic greetings, and "thank you," and "two months."
<v Laura Moore>学了一点。只是一些基本的问候，还有“谢谢”，和“两个月”。

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:47.010
<v Adam Davidson>Say all that stuff. Say it.
<v Adam Davidson>说说看。说吧。

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:52.480
<v Laura Moore>Two months? [SPEAKING ARABIC], come back in two months.
<v Laura Moore>两个月？[阿拉伯语发言]，两个月后再来。

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:59.320
<v Adam Davidson>After watching her for 45 minutes, my translator Amjad and I sit under a tree to get out of the sun.
<v Adam Davidson>观察了她45分钟后，我的翻译阿姆贾德和我坐在树下躲避太阳。

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:09.490
<v Adam Davidson>Amjad, you seem very angry right now.
<v Adam Davidson>阿姆贾德，你现在看起来很生气。

00:50:09.490 --> 00:50:29.760
<v Amjad>When I saw these people, it immediately comes to my mind what was the Iraqi suffering during Saddam Hussein. You can find the same queues asking for jobs, asking their government to give them back their properties. The same things, no difference.
<v Amjad>当我看到这些人时，我脑子里立刻出现了伊拉克人在萨达姆·侯赛因统治下遭受的苦难。你可以发现同样的排队求职，求政府归还他们的财产。同样的事情，没区别。

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:31.450
<v Adam Davidson>And are the Americans more helpful?
<v Adam Davidson>而美国人有帮助吗？

00:50:31.450 --> 00:50:47.560
<v Amjad>No, they're following the same procedures. Come tomorrow, come after one month, come after two months and we'll see. We'll find out what we can do for you. And I'm sure, I'm positively sure, that they can't do anything for them.
<v Amjad>不，他们遵循同样的程序。明天来，一个月后再来，两个月后再来，我们会看看。我们会弄清楚我们能为你做什么。而且我肯定，我绝对肯定，他们对他们什么都做不了。

00:50:47.560 --> 00:51:18.760
<v Adam Davidson>Privately, some US officials say things didn't need to get this bad, that the gap between Iraqis and Americans didn't need to be so vast, that we should have done in Iraq what we've done after other wars. Once victory was declared, Americans needed to be out in the streets in big numbers listening to Iraqis, holding town meetings, letting Iraqis see them and get to know them, building trust. But because of security concerns-- soldiers are still shot at every day in Iraq. Over 40 have died since the war officially ended-- Americans aren't doing that.
<v Adam Davidson>私下里，一些美国官员说事情本来不至于变得这么糟糕，伊拉克人和美国人之间的差距本来不至于如此巨大，我们本来应该在伊拉克做我们在其他战争后做过的事。一旦胜利宣布，美国人需要成群结队地走上街头听取伊拉克人的声音，召开市政会议，让伊拉克人看到他们并了解他们，建立信任。但因为安全问题——士兵们在伊拉克每天仍在遭到枪击。自战争正式结束以来已有40多人死亡——美国人没有那样做。

00:51:18.760 --> 00:51:28.720
<v Sheik>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:51:28.720 --> 00:52:06.410
<v Adam Davidson>Curious to see what happens when Americans try to bridge the information gap and meet directly with Iraqis, I sat in as a group of influential Iraqis met with some American representatives. It wasn't very promising. This was a group of tribal sheiks who are more powerful in Iraq then you'd probably think. Though Iraq is a modern country with a lot of educated people and a big middle class, most Iraqis belong to a tribe. And every tribe has a sheik. A sheik can call on his people to do all sorts of things, to boycott something or to march. It's the sheik who decides when a tribe will pick up arms and go to battle. Even Saddam Hussein was scared enough of these guys to meet with them and listen to them.
<v Adam Davidson>出于好奇想看看当美国人试图弥合信息差距并直接与伊拉克人会面时会发生什么，我旁听了一次有影响力的伊拉克人与一些美国代表的会面。这并不怎么有希望。这是一群部落酋长，他们在伊拉克的影响力比你可能想到的要大。虽然伊拉克是一个现代化国家，有很多受过教育的人和庞大的中产阶级，但大多数伊拉克人属于某个部落。每个部落都有一个酋长。酋长可以号召他的人民做各种各样的事情，抵制某事或游行。是酋长决定部落何时拿起武器去战斗。即使是萨达姆·侯赛因也对这些人感到恐惧，以至于要会见他们并听取他们的意见。

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:23.030
<v Adam Davidson>This is the third meeting like this for the sheiks. A few hundred of them representing most of Iraq's tribes sit in a big theater. At a table on the stage is Hume Horan, counsel to the head of the US coalition, Paul Bremer. Horan speaks Arabic quite well. And he's sitting next to five sheiks.
<v Adam Davidson>这是酋长们第三次进行这样的会面。几百名代表伊拉克大部分部落的酋长坐在一个大剧院里。舞台上的桌边是休姆·霍兰，美国联盟首脑保罗·布雷默的顾问。霍兰的阿拉伯语说得很好。他旁边坐着五位酋长。

00:52:23.030 --> 00:52:26.860
<v Sheik>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:52:26.860 --> 00:52:55.540
<v Adam Davidson>The sheiks on stage are among the few who have strongly allied themselves with the Americans. They each give a speech about how much good the Americans have done, how all the sheiks have to stay unified and work with the Americans. No sheik should start any trouble. They're interrupted all the time by sheiks in the audience screaming out questions and complaints. The sheiks on stage tell them not to interrupt, that all their questions will be answered by the speeches that are being given. Everyone will see that all their problems are already being taken care of.
<v Adam Davidson>台上的酋长是少数强烈与美国人结盟的人。他们每个人都发表演讲，称赞美国人做了多少好事，所有酋长必须保持团结并与美国人合作。没有任何酋长应该惹事生非。他们一直被台下大声尖叫提出问题和抱怨的酋长打断。台上的酋长告诉他们不要打断，所有问题都会在即将发表的演讲中得到回答。每个人都会看到他们所有的问题都已经被解决了。

00:52:55.540 --> 00:52:57.590
<v Hume Horan>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Hume Horan>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:52:57.590 --> 00:53:27.360
<v Adam Davidson>In his speech, Horan keeps it general and upbeat. He tells the crowd that he hopes many of them will become his close friends. He says the sheiks' concerns, electricity, security, water, are not a big problem. The Americans have done so much good, he says. Iraq is much safer now. There's plenty of electricity. And anyway, none of it is America's fault. All the problems Iraqis face these days are because of all the bad things Saddam did when he was in power. But the Americans are on the job, and everything's fine now, and we'll get better soon.
<v Adam Davidson>在演讲中，霍兰保持了概括性和乐观。他告诉人群，希望他们中的许多人成为他的密友。他说酋长们关心的电、安全、水，都不是什么大问题。他说美国人做了那么多好事。伊拉克现在安全多了。电力充足。无论如何，这些都不是美国的错。伊拉克人这些天面临的所有问题都是因为萨达姆掌权时做的所有坏事。但美国人正在工作中，现在一切都很好，我们会很快好起来。

00:53:27.360 --> 00:53:29.200
<v Hume Horan>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Hume Horan>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:53:29.200 --> 00:54:03.970
<v Adam Davidson>Nobody interrupts him. But as soon as he's done talking and one of the onstage sheiks starts another speech, congratulating Horan for his words, the audience sheiks are standing up, screaming out that there is no security. Iraq is less safe now than ever. Electricity is completely unreliable. The sheiks onstage are telling them to be quiet. One says "Be polite. We are Arabs. We are a polite people." But the audience sheiks won't stop shouting out their complaints. One of the onstage sheiks finally says to a man in the audience who won't sit down, "Dear sir, go ahead. You're welcome to speak."
<v Adam Davidson>没人打断他。但当他讲完，台上一位酋长开始另一场演讲，祝贺霍兰的言论时，台下的酋长们站了起来，尖叫着说根本没有安全。伊拉克现在比以往任何时候都更不安全。电力完全不可靠。台上的酋长们叫他们安静。一个人说：“礼貌点。我们是阿拉伯人。我们是有礼貌的民族。”但台下的酋长们不会停止大喊他们的抱怨。台上的一位酋长最终对台下一个不肯坐下的男人说：“亲爱的先生，请便。欢迎发言。”

00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:20.520
<v Adam Davidson>At this point, the meeting is pretty much over as an organized event. One sheik after another stands up and screams out how badly things are going under the Americans. Horan and the sheiks onstage never respond to these impromptu speeches. They just sit on the stage and watch the audience take over.
<v Adam Davidson>此时，这次会议作为一场有组织的活动已经基本结束了。一位接一位酋长站起来尖叫着美军统治下的事情有多糟糕。霍兰和台上的酋长们从未回应这些即兴演讲。他们只是坐在台上看着台下的人接管了一切。

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:31.150
<v Adam Davidson>[AUDIENCE SOUNDS]
<v Adam Davidson>[听众声音]

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:37.540
<v Adam Davidson>How did you think the meeting went? Did you feel the Americans were listening to you? Were they responsive to what you were saying?
<v Adam Davidson>你觉得这次会议进行得怎么样？你觉得美国人在听你们说吗？他们对你们所说的话有回应吗？

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:41.090
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:44.100
<v Translator>I don't think they are responding to these demands.
<v Translator>我不认为他们正在回应这些需求。

00:54:44.100 --> 00:55:11.910
<v Adam Davidson>One of the angriest sheiks in the audience is Faisal [? Rakan ?] [? Nisriz ?] al-Goud, the head of a large family in the Dulaim tribe, the biggest tribe in Iraq with 5 million members. Goud says he came to the meeting completely pro-American. He loves George Bush. But because of meetings like this one, the sheiks are divided off into those who want to declare war on the Americans immediately and moderates like him, who want to give the Americans one more month to see their true intentions before declaring war.
<v Adam Davidson>台下最愤怒的酋长之一是费萨尔·拉坎·尼斯里兹·阿尔-古德，杜莱姆部落一个大家族的领袖，这是伊拉克最大的部落，有500万成员。古德说他来参加会议时是完全亲美的。他爱乔治·布什。但因为像这样的会议，酋长们分成了那些想要立即向美国人宣战的人，以及像他这样想要再给美国人一个月时间看看他们真实意图再宣战的温和派。

00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:19.440
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:40.940
<v Translator>By then I think all heads of tribes and members of tribes will start attacking the coalition forces by every means, whether by grenades, clashing [? coast ?], anything to fight the coalition, the US presence in Iraq.
<v Translator>到时候我想所有部落的首领和成员都会开始以各种手段攻击联盟部队，无论是手榴弹、冲突，任何手段来对抗联盟，美国在伊拉克的存在。

00:55:40.940 --> 00:55:46.330
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:49.350
<v Translator>No more than one month.
<v Translator>不超过一个月。

00:55:49.350 --> 00:55:51.950
<v Adam Davidson>Are you ready to fight in a month?
<v Adam Davidson>你准备好在一个月后战斗了吗？

00:55:51.950 --> 00:56:01.030
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Sheik Faisal Al-goud>[阿拉伯语发言]

00:56:01.030 --> 00:56:09.830
<v Translator>We are a united people. And we stand by each other in facing good or bad things.
<v Translator>我们是一个团结的民族。无论面临好事还是坏事，我们都站在一起。

00:56:09.830 --> 00:56:41.130
<v Adam Davidson>It's hard to tell how serious this threat is. Things seem to get more violent each week in Iraq as people get more frustrated. Either way, it's not good. The sheiks came to a meeting that the Americans hoped would get across our message of hope, that we're in Iraq to rebuild, to help, not to stay. And Goud and the other sheiks came out of it madder than ever, talking about war.
<v Adam Davidson>很难说这个威胁有多严重。伊拉克的情况似乎每周都变得更加暴力，因为人们变得越来越沮丧。无论怎样，这都不好。酋长们来到一个美国人希望传递我们希望信息的会议，即我们来伊拉克是为了重建，为了帮助，而不是为了留下。而古德和其他酋长从会议中出来时比以往任何时候都更愤怒，谈论着战争。

00:56:41.130 --> 00:57:13.670
<v Adam Davidson>In my last week in Baghdad, my translator, Amjad, and I had lunch with a friend of his who was telling funny stories about how, as a little boy, he actually met Saddam Hussein. Saddam used to invite kids to the palace and hand out toys in big photo ops for the newspapers to show what a great guy he was. Amjad laughed. "It's just like Bremer," he said. The head of the US operation, Paul Bremer, had just invited the press to photograph him handing out soccer balls to young people. "You know what we call Bremer?" Amjad's friend asked me. "Bremer Hussein."
<v Adam Davidson>在我在巴格达的最后一周，我的翻译阿姆贾德和我与他的一位朋友共进午餐，他讲了一些有趣的关于他小时候如何真的见过萨达姆·侯赛因的故事。萨达姆过去常邀请孩子们去宫殿并分发玩具，作为报纸的大型拍照机会，以展示他是一个多么伟大的人。阿姆贾德笑了。“这就像布雷默，”他说。美军行动的首脑保罗·布雷默，刚刚邀请媒体拍摄他分发足球给年轻人的照片。“你知道我们怎么叫布雷默吗？”阿姆贾德的朋友问我。“布雷默·侯赛因。”

00:57:13.670 --> 00:57:42.200
<v Adam Davidson>Which brings me back to that weird rumor about Saddam and why it's so popular, the rumor that he worked with the Americans, arranged for the invasion, and now lives in the White House. For all the important differences between American rule and Saddam's government, Iraqis say they're seeing some of the same things. The country is still run by reclusive men who make decisions in private and issue their orders from behind the guarded walls of the Republican Palace.
<v Adam Davidson>这让我想起了关于萨达姆的那个奇怪的谣言，为什么它如此流行，那个他与美国人合作、安排了入侵、现在住在白宫的谣言。尽管美军统治与萨达姆政府之间存在所有重要的区别，但伊拉克人说他们看到了一些同样的东西。国家仍然由深居简出的人运行，他们在私下里做出决定，并从共和国宫守卫森严的墙后发布命令。

00:57:42.200 --> 00:58:13.700
<v Ira Glass>Adam Davidson is a regular contributor to our show and has recently been Marketplace 's correspondent in Baghdad.
<v Ira Glass>亚当·戴维森是我们节目的定期撰稿人，最近一直是《市场》在巴格达的通讯员。

00:58:13.700 --> 00:58:31.410
<v Ira Glass>Well, our website, www.thisamericanlife.org, where you can listen to our shows for free or buy tapes. Or you know you can download audio of our program at audible.com/thisamericanlife, where they have public radio programs, bestselling books, even the New York Times , all at audible.com.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的网站，www.thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费收听我们的节目或购买磁带。或者你可以从audible.com/thisamericanlife下载我们节目的音频，那里有公共广播节目，畅销书，甚至《纽约时报》，都在audible.com。

00:58:31.410 --> 00:58:46.280
<v Ira Glass>Alex Meyer, the teenager from the beginning of our program, is at alexmeyer.tk. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who has just one request.
<v Ira Glass>节目开始时的少年亚历克斯·梅耶在alexmeyer.tk。《这就是美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。WBEZ的管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责，他只有一个请求。

00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:51.560
<v David Sedaris>There's a little gap down by the baseboard. Bend over and close it, will you?
<v David Sedaris>踢脚板那里有个小缝。弯腰把它关上，好吗？

00:58:51.560 --> 00:58:56.920
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life .
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周再见，会有更多《这就是美国生活》的故事。

00:58:56.920 --> 00:59:01.920
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
